INT. BELL TOWER - CHURCH - DAY

1944. Dust and pigeon droppings. A cracked bronze bell hangs dead overhead. Through an arched opening, a ruined village smokes.

SERGEANT ILARIO COSTA, 30s, unshaven, rifle across his lap, sits against the wall eating a tin of sardines. Peaceful. Birds sing outside.

A scrape on the stairs. Costa doesn't look up. He eases the safety off.

A WOUNDED SOLDIER, 19, ragged, one arm in a bloodstained sling, climbs into view. He freezes, hands raised.

WOUNDED SOLDIER
Don't shoot. Please. I'm lost.

COSTA
Which side?

WOUNDED SOLDIER
Yours. I think.

COSTA
You think.

He lowers the rifle an inch. The soldier sags against the stair rail, relieved. Costa offers him the sardine tin. The soldier takes it, eats with trembling fingers.

COSTA (CONT'D)
Name?

TADEUSZ
Tadeusz Wolak. Polish Second Corps.

COSTA
Where's your unit, Tadeusz?

TADEUSZ
(still chewing)
Below. Looking for you.

Costa's face goes flat. Far down the stairwell, boots. Many.

TADEUSZ (CONT'D)
I'm sorry, Sergeant. They said they'd spare my brother.
